---
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import Explainer from '../../../components/Explainer.astro'
import { snippets } from '../../../data/components.js'
import { diagrams } from '../../../data/diagrams.js'
import { recordingChecks } from '../../../data/release.js'
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/paths.js'

const repo = 'https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut/blob/main'

const record = `#include <suitecut/browser.hpp>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>

using suitecut::Json;

suitecut::LaunchOptions options;
options.executable = "/absolute/path/to/suitecut-browser";
options.frames_per_second = 30; // capture cadence
auto browser = suitecut::Browser::launch(options);
auto page = browser.main_page();
page.navigate("https://example.com/");

auto started = page.command("recording", Json{
    {"action", "start"}, {"ffmpegPath", "/usr/bin/ffmpeg"},
    {"outputPath", "/tmp/page.webm"}, {"framesPerSecond", 30},
    {"width", 1280}, {"height", 720}, {"audio", true}});
std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(5));
auto result = page.command("recording", Json{
    {"action", "stop"}, {"recordingId", started.at("id")}});
browser.close();`

const chromium = `suitecut::LaunchOptions options;
options.executable = "/usr/bin/chromium";
auto browser = suitecut::Browser::launch_chromium(options);
auto page = browser.main_page();
page.navigate("https://example.com/");
page.start_recording("/tmp/page.mp4", 30, suitecut::Json{
    {"ffmpegPath", "/usr/bin/ffmpeg"}, {"ffprobePath", "/usr/bin/ffprobe"}});
// ...
auto result = page.stop_recording();`

const stream = `auto started = page.command("streaming", suitecut::Json{
    {"action", "start"}, {"clientToken", "stream-1"},
    {"ffmpegPath", "/usr/bin/ffmpeg"}, {"destination", "rtmp://127.0.0.1/live/key"},
    {"framesPerSecond", 30}, {"width", 1280}, {"height", 720}, {"audio", true},
    {"videoCodec", "h264"}, {"audioCodec", "aac"}, {"container", "flv"},
    {"bitrateKbps", 4500}});
const auto id = started.at("id");
auto status = page.command("streaming", suitecut::Json{{"action", "status"}, {"streamId", id}});
auto finished = page.command("streaming", suitecut::Json{{"action", "stop"}, {"streamId", id}});`

const reconnect = `{"reconnect": {"maxAttempts": 3, "initialDelayMs": 250, "maxDelayMs": 5000, "timeoutMs": 30000}}`

type Row = readonly [string, string]

const recordingActions: Row[] = [
  ['start', 'ffmpegPath, outputPath, framesPerSecond, width, height, audio; optional maxFileBytes'],
  ['status', 'Current counters for the recording'],
  ['pause, resume', 'Pause the recording at the command time, and resume it'],
  ['stop', 'Finish the file and report its counters'],
  ['cancel', 'Cancels the recording'],
]

const streamingActions: Row[] = [
  ['start', 'Starts a publisher; replies once encoded output is observed'],
  ['status', 'Counters, phase, the selected page and the output rate'],
  ['pause, resume', 'Holds the image and sends silence while the live timeline continues'],
  ['select', 'Switches the source to another page in the same engine (sourcePageId)'],
  ['stop', 'Waits for the encoder to finish and reports the final counters'],
  ['cancel', 'Cancels the publisher'],
]
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Recording and streaming"
  description="Record a page to WebM or MP4 with its sound, or publish it live over RTMP, RTMPS or TCP, from the suitecut engine."
  section="recording"
>
  <h1>Recording and streaming</h1>
  <p>
    The engine records a page with its sound into a file, or publishes it live. Both are engine
    commands, so they work the same from C++, from the C ABI with <code>sc_client_call</code>, or
    from anything that writes the engine's JSON lines. FFmpeg does the muxing; on macOS an MP4 uses
    the hardware H.264 encoder.
  </p>

  <h2 id="record">Record a page</h2>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.recording} />
  <CodeBlock code={record} label="C++" lang="cpp" />
  <p>
    The engine's <code>recording</code> command takes these actions. Every action but
    <code>start</code> names the recording with <code>recordingId</code>, and any field the command
    doesn't know is rejected.
  </p>
  <ul class="dp-rows">
    {
      recordingActions.map(([key, value]) => (
        <li>
          <code>{key}</code>
          <span class="dp-detail">{value}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    The output is WebM, or MP4 on macOS with hardware encoding. Width and height must be even, and
    page audio is optional. <code>framesPerSecond</code> may be fractional, such as 29.97: capture runs
    at the whole-number launch option <code>frames_per_second</code>, and the encoder resamples onto
    the requested timeline. A higher output rate repeats captured images; it doesn't create motion
    the browser didn't paint. <a href={sitePath('/docs/how-it-works#clock')}>One clock</a> explains how
    frames are picked and held.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recorder">The bundled recorder</h2>
  <p>
    <code>suitecut-native-record</code> is the program above with flags. It finds the engine, FFmpeg and
    FFprobe relative to its own location in an installed runtime.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={snippets.recordCode} label="Terminal" lang="bash" />

  <h2 id="chromium">Recording Chrome or Chromium</h2>
  <p>
    <code>Page::start_recording</code> records an installed Chrome or Chromium. Source frames are spooled,
    resampled to the output rate and finalized with FFmpeg and FFprobe. The result is a silent MP4 or
    WebM; page audio needs the engine.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={chromium} label="C++" lang="cpp" />

  <h2 id="stream">Stream a page live</h2>
  <Explainer explained={diagrams.streaming} />
  <p>
    The <code>streaming</code> command publishes over <code>rtmp://</code>, <code>rtmps://</code> or
    <code>tcp://</code>. The engine does frame-rate conversion, the FFmpeg process, pipe
    backpressure and the publisher's lifetime; the caller sends commands and reads counters. It runs
    on Linux and macOS.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={stream} label="C++" lang="cpp" />
  <ul class="dp-rows">
    {
      streamingActions.map(([key, value]) => (
        <li>
          <code>{key}</code>
          <span class="dp-detail">{value}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>start</code> requires every field shown above. Codecs are <code>h264</code> and
    <code>aac</code> in <code>flv</code>, or <code>vp9</code> and <code>opus</code> in
    <code>webm</code>; RTMP and RTMPS need the first pair. The bitrate is fixed for the life of the
    publisher. Encoded and muxed counters show local FFmpeg progress, not receipt by the server.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reconnect">Reconnecting</h2>
  <p>
    Without <code>reconnect</code> the engine makes one publisher attempt. With it, the engine replaces
    a failed publisher, backing off between attempts. These are the defaults:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={reconnect} label="JSON" lang="json" />
  <p>
    Between attempts the phase is <code>reconnecting</code>. While disconnected, only the latest
    picture is kept and captured audio is dropped and counted. Cancellation, a page crash and
    pipe-lag failures stay terminal.
  </p>

  <h2 id="timing">Timing</h2>
  <p>
    With audio, the publisher buffers 120 ms of captured media by default (<code
      >captureLatencyMs</code
    >). Audio advances in 20 ms chunks on a 48 kHz timeline; audio that isn't there by its playout
    time becomes counted silence. <code>maxWriteLagMs</code> (default 2000) fails a publisher whose pipe
    falls that far behind.
  </p>

  <h2 id="known-issues">Known issues in 0.1.1</h2>
  <p>The 0.1.1 release recordings on macOS ARM64 came out like this:</p>
  <ul class="dp-rows">
    <li>
      <code>.mp4</code>
      <span class="dp-detail">{recordingChecks.mp4}</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.webm</code>
      <span class="dp-detail">{recordingChecks.webm}</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Both containers now start a recording with audio at CEF's first audio packet. A <code
      >.webm</code
    >
    recording, which uses software VP9 and Opus, opens within about 30 ms; a <code>.mp4</code>
    recording, which uses VideoToolbox H.264 and AAC, within about 10 ms. In 0.1.0 a <code
      >.webm</code
    >
    recording opened with about 140 ms of silence.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recording throughput depends on the encoder and on machine load. Asked for 120 FPS, software VP9
    kept 120 distinct frames per second up to 2560×1440 and 71 at 3840×2160 in the release run,
    while hardware H.264 kept 120 up to 3840×2160. In one 0.1.0 run a 2560×1440 <code>.webm</code> recording
    failed under load with
    <code>Native encoder video pipe failed</code>, and passed when run alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing is notarized yet, so Gatekeeper blocks a downloaded copy of the embed kit's or the
    runtime's app until it is.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <p>
    A decoded-window audit still finds tone dropouts, and low-rate Opus startup can fail; continuous
    audio and A/V synchronization are still being repaired. Switching to a page in another engine
    and changing the bitrate while live aren't supported. Live streaming throughput isn't measured;
    the measured recording numbers are on the <a href={sitePath('/docs/performance')}
      >performance page</a
    >. The full reference is <a href={`${repo}/docs/native-publishing.md`}
      >native continuous publishing</a
    >.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
